sequenceDiagram
    autonumber

    box rgb(240,241,243) Outside the cluster
        participant LG as Load generator<br/>42 requests per second
    end
    box rgb(253,240,239) The injected fault
        participant CH as Chaos command<br/>docker kill node C
    end
    box rgb(238,243,253) The orchestrator
        participant KCP as Kubernetes<br/>control plane
    end
    box rgb(237,247,247) The application under test
        participant GW as API Gateway<br/>5 s request budget
        participant SVC as order-backend Service<br/>the load balancer
        participant SV as Replicas 1 and 2<br/>nodes A and B
        participant R3 as Third replica<br/>node C, then a fresh copy
    end
    box rgb(238,243,253) Observability and AI
        participant OT as OpenTelemetry<br/>and Jaeger
        participant ML as Recovery-time<br/>model
    end

    rect rgb(237,247,247)
    Note over LG,ML: PHASE 0 - HEALTHY BASELINE. Three replicas, 70 percent of capacity.
    LG->>GW: Order requests, 42 every second
    GW->>SVC: Forward each request on a new connection
    SVC->>SV: Two thirds of the traffic
    SVC->>R3: One third of the traffic
    R3-->>GW: 200 OK, median 110 ms
    GW->>OT: Trace: healthy, with the serving pod and node recorded
    Note over SV,R3: Each replica serves 2 requests at a time, 0.1 s each,<br/>so 20 per second. Three replicas give 60 per second.
    end

    rect rgb(253,240,239)
    Note over LG,ML: PHASE 1 - BLACK HOLE, about 45 s. Kubernetes does not know the node is gone.
    CH->>R3: Kill node C instantly, 60 s into the run
    LG->>GW: Requests continue
    GW->>SVC: Forward each request
    SVC-xR3: One third of the traffic is still routed to the dead node
    R3--xGW: No reply. The gateway gives up after 5 s.
    GW->>OT: Trace: ERROR, upstream timeout
    Note right of GW: One request in three fails, yet the requests that<br/>reach a living replica are still fast. That mixture is<br/>what makes the fault hard to read.
    KCP->>SVC: 40 s of missed heartbeats. Remove the dead endpoint.
    end

    rect rgb(255,247,237)
    Note over LG,ML: PHASE 2 - BROWNOUT. Two replicas absorb all of the traffic.
    LG->>GW: Requests continue
    GW->>SVC: Forward each request
    SVC->>SV: All of the traffic
    SV-->>GW: 200 OK, but the queue grows. Median 110 ms climbs to about 1,900 ms.
    GW->>OT: Trace: healthy, but latency is heavily degraded
    Note right of SV: 42 requests per second against 40 per second of<br/>capacity. There are no failures at all now, yet the<br/>service is roughly 15 times slower. An alarm that<br/>watches only the error rate reports success.
    end

    rect rgb(237,247,247)
    Note over LG,ML: PHASE 3 - RECOVERY
    KCP->>R3: Node C is gone, so start a fresh copy on a surviving node
    Note right of R3: The new copy refuses traffic for its first 15 s while it<br/>warms up, so a replacement is scheduled long before<br/>it is of any use.
    SVC->>R3: Readiness probe: can you serve traffic yet?
    R3-->>SVC: Ready. Add me back to the endpoints.
    SVC->>R3: Traffic spreads across three replicas again
    R3-->>GW: 200 OK. The backlog drains and latency falls back to baseline.
    GW->>OT: Trace: healthy, back to about 110 ms
    end

    rect rgb(238,243,253)
    Note over OT,ML: PREDICTING THE REMAINING RECOVERY TIME
    OT->>ML: One row per second: request rate, error rate, median and<br/>95th percentile latency, replicas serving, latency slope
    Note over ML: Recovered means the 95th percentile is back within 25 percent<br/>of baseline, errors are back to normal, and all three replicas<br/>are serving, held for 8 of the next 10 seconds.
    ML-->>LG: "Node failure. About 103 s to full recovery." MAE 13.1 s, R-squared 0.815
    end
